# Line endings are part of this repository's compiled output, not a local
# preference. Seven `include_dir!` trees and ~95 `include_str!`/`include_bytes!`
# targets are read from the working tree at compile time, so whatever line
# endings a checkout has are baked into every binary built from it. Git for
# Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which without this file materialises
# all 1250 tracked text files as CRLF and compiles that into the artifact.
#
# The index is already uniformly LF, so this file changes no committed bytes; it
# pins what a checkout materialises. It is also why the `strip_prefix("---\n")`
# family of frontmatter parsers keeps being found one at a time — the parsers
# stay CRLF-tolerant for foreign trees, but this repo's own bytes are ours to
# pin.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Reproduced third-party licence text, verbatim. 1752 of its line endings are
# CRLF because the upstream licence files they came from are CRLF, and
# `navigator ops notices --check` compares the committed file to freshly
# rendered upstream text byte-for-byte (`cli/src/notices.rs:376`, `current ==
# rendered`). Normalising it would both break that required check and alter
# licence text we reproduce rather than author.
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt -text

# Binary assets. Git already auto-detects these; naming them keeps a future
# mostly-text asset from being normalised on the strength of that guess.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf   binary
*.ico   binary

# This one is named explicitly, not left to `*.pdf` above. Its 621 bytes
# contain no NUL, so Git's auto-detection calls it text and `core.autocrlf`
# injects 36 CRs on checkout, shifting every offset past the first newline
# while its `startxref 438` still points at the old one -- a structurally
# invalid PDF in the working tree. Naming it keeps that repair from being
# undone by a future narrowing of the glob.
store/seeds/dev/initial-case-assessment.pdf binary
